---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import MermaidDiagram from '../components/MermaidDiagram.astro';
import { mcpServersDiagram } from '../data/diagrams';
---

<DocsLayout title="MCP Servers" sectionLabel="How It Works" activeSlug="mcp-servers">

  <div class="panel-intro">
    <h2>MCP Servers</h2>
    <p>MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers give agents access to <strong>external tools and services</strong> &mdash; documentation lookup, browser automation, database inspection, payment APIs, and more. VibeCrew ships with bundled servers and a registry of additional ones that get activated on demand once the project's architecture is clear.</p>
    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>What they are</strong>
          <p>Lightweight tool servers that run alongside Claude Code via <code>npx</code>. Each server exposes a set of tools (search docs, run browser, query database) that agents can call during their work. Configured in <code>.mcp.json</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
          <p>Without MCP servers, agents would paste entire library docs into the context window (~5,000+ tokens per lookup). Context7 replaces that with a single tool call (~1,500 tokens saved per query). Service-specific servers (Supabase, Stripe) let agents inspect schemas and APIs directly instead of guessing from docs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── On-Demand Architecture ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>On-Demand Integration</h3>
    <p>VibeCrew doesn't load all MCP servers upfront. Only 3 general-purpose servers are enabled by default. The rest activate <strong>after the TDR (Technology Decision Record) is finalized</strong> during Tier 1, when VibeCrew knows exactly which technologies the project uses.</p>

    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon blue">&#9654;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>How auto-discovery works</strong>
          <p>After TDR approval, <code>sync-mcp-from-tdr.sh</code> reads the technology decisions, pattern-matches them against the MCP registry, and auto-enables matching servers. If the TDR mentions "Supabase," the Supabase MCP server activates. If it mentions "Stripe," payment tools appear. Servers that need environment variables (API keys) are flagged for setup.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon amber">&#9888;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Why wait for architecture?</strong>
          <p>Loading unused MCP servers wastes tool-discovery tokens and clutters the agent's tool list. Claude Code's Tool Search dynamically loads only relevant tool definitions when 5+ servers are active &mdash; but fewer active servers still means faster routing and lower overhead.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Default Servers ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Default Servers (Always Enabled)</h3>
    <p>These 3 servers are useful for any project regardless of tech stack. They're enabled in <code>.mcp.json</code> from the start.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Used By</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Context7</strong></td>
            <td>Up-to-date documentation lookup for libraries and frameworks. Replaces pasting docs into context (~1,500 tokens saved per query).</td>
            <td>Stack Scout, Builder, all research tasks</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Chrome DevTools</strong></td>
            <td>Browser automation and inspection via Chrome DevTools Protocol. Navigate pages, inspect elements, capture screenshots.</td>
            <td>Builder (visual verification), E2E testing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Playwright</strong></td>
            <td>End-to-end browser testing and automation. Drives real browsers for integration tests.</td>
            <td><code>/e2e</code>, Builder (component testing)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Conditional Servers ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Conditional Servers (TDR-Activated)</h3>
    <p>These 6 servers ship in <code>.mcp.json</code> but start disabled. They activate automatically when the TDR selects matching technologies, or manually via <code>bash scripts/enable-mcp-server.sh &lt;name&gt; enable</code>.</p>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Requires</th><th>TDR Trigger</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Semgrep</strong></td>
            <td>Static analysis and code security scanning</td>
            <td>&mdash;</td>
            <td>Any project (security audit)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Sentry</strong></td>
            <td>Error tracking and performance monitoring</td>
            <td><code>SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN</code></td>
            <td>TDR mentions "sentry"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Supabase</strong></td>
            <td>PostgreSQL, auth, storage, real-time subscriptions</td>
            <td><code>SUPABASE_URL</code>, <code>SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY</code></td>
            <td>TDR mentions "supabase"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stripe</strong></td>
            <td>Payment processing, subscriptions, invoicing</td>
            <td><code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code></td>
            <td>TDR mentions "stripe" or "payments"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Vercel</strong></td>
            <td>Deployment, serverless functions, edge config</td>
            <td><code>VERCEL_TOKEN</code></td>
            <td>TDR mentions "vercel"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Figma</strong></td>
            <td>Design file access and design-to-code workflows</td>
            <td><code>FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN</code></td>
            <td>TDR mentions "figma"</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Extended Registry ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Extended Registry</h3>
    <p>Beyond the bundled servers, <code>templates/mcp-registry.json</code> contains additional servers that can be auto-discovered from TDR technology choices. When a match is found, VibeCrew recommends the server and provides setup instructions.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Database &amp; ORM</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Requires</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Firebase</strong></td><td>Firestore, auth, hosting, cloud functions</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Prisma</strong></td><td>Type-safe ORM for PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>MongoDB</strong></td><td>Document database with Atlas cloud and Mongoose ODM</td><td><code>MDB_MCP_CONNECTION_STRING</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Neon</strong></td><td>Serverless PostgreSQL with branching and autoscaling</td><td><code>NEON_API_KEY</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Upstash Redis</strong></td><td>Serverless Redis and Kafka for caching and messaging</td><td><code>UPSTASH_EMAIL</code>, <code>UPSTASH_API_KEY</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Authentication</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Requires</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Clerk</strong></td><td>Authentication and user management with prebuilt UI</td><td><code>CLERK_SECRET_KEY</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Auth0</strong></td><td>Identity platform with SSO, MFA, social login</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Hosting, Frameworks &amp; Infrastructure</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Requires</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Netlify</strong></td><td>Web hosting, serverless functions, edge deployment</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Railway</strong></td><td>Infrastructure platform for deploying apps and databases</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Shopify</strong></td><td>E-commerce platform and Hydrogen framework</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Next.js DevTools</strong></td><td>Next.js development tools and route inspection</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>shadcn/ui</strong></td><td>Component library with Tailwind CSS and Radix UI</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Terraform</strong></td><td>Infrastructure as Code for cloud provisioning</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes</strong></td><td>Container orchestration and cluster management</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Resend</strong></td><td>Email API for transactional and marketing emails</td><td><code>RESEND_API_KEY</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Fallback & Health ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Fallback &amp; Health Monitoring</h3>
    <p>All MCP servers are <strong>optional</strong>. If a server fails to connect, crashes, or isn't configured, agents gracefully fall back to built-in tools. No MCP server is required for VibeCrew to function.</p>
    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Graceful degradation</strong>
          <p>If the Supabase MCP is unavailable, the Builder reads migration files instead of inspecting the live schema. If Context7 is down, agents use WebSearch. Every agent's prompt includes explicit fallback instructions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon amber">&#9888;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Health probes</strong>
          <p><code>check-mcp-health.sh</code> sends a JSON-RPC <code>initialize</code> probe to each enabled server with a 2-second timeout. Failed servers are reported as "degraded" in <code>/status</code> output.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <MermaidDiagram chart={mcpServersDiagram} caption="MCP server activation flow: from TDR decisions to enabled tool servers" />

</DocsLayout>
